{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "The point of a prototype is to learn quickly whether an idea is worth pursuing, and the code is meant to be thrown away, so reading it closely is mostly wasted effort. Keep it isolated from real data, credentials, and production systems, and don't let it quietly turn into the MVP codebase."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "A demo mainly has to work reliably along the path you will show, so judging it by behavior is usually enough. Be honest with stakeholders that it is a demo and not a foundation. If it handles real customer data or secrets, step down to level 3 for those parts."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Real users mean real data, security exposure, and code you will have to build on, so unread code becomes a liability. Have AI write the code, but review closely the areas where mistakes are costly (auth, payments, data handling, data model and schema, external integrations), skim the rest, and depend on automated tests for critical user flows."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A team maintaining a paid product needs every merged change to be understood by at least one person, so the usual code review standard should apply to AI-written code just as it does to human-written code. Review closely for security-sensitive, data-migration, and core-domain changes (effectively level 2 there), and back everything with CI, tests, and the ability to roll back."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, every line must be understood, traceable, and defensible to auditors, so AI can draft code but people must review every change line by line under the existing verification and compliance process. For the most sensitive components (safety interlocks, cryptography, certified modules), or where regulations or certification limit tool use, drop to level 1 or 0."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Human scrutiny should scale with the cost of being wrong and with how long the code will live. Vibe code freely when the code is disposable (prototypes and demos), move to selective review plus tests once real users and data are involved, and require line-by-line human review where failures are regulated or dangerous. At every stage, apply the stricter level to high-risk areas such as auth, payments, personal data, and migrations no matter what stage the product is in, and treat a project moving to a new stage as a reason to re-review or rewrite code that was written under looser rules."
}

